"""Home & Personal Finance toolkit (kit id home-money): household budget, loan EMI tracker, bills and subscriptions,
   family documents index, house-help salary register, home inventory, and the online home budget planner.
   Record-keeping only: no investment advice anywhere. Files are named HM-*.xlsx so they never clash with other kits.
   `previews` = {file: (sheet, range)} for the page preview (build.py PREVIEW has only the first five toolkits' files)."""

KITS = [
    dict(id='home-money', name='Home & Personal Finance', icon='🏠', color='#DB2777',
         title='Home & Personal Finance Toolkit: Household Budget, Loan EMI and Bills Tracker Templates',
         desc='Free household budget, loan EMI and prepayment tracker, bills and subscription due dates, family documents index, house-help salary register and home inventory for Excel.',
         intro=['Running a home is a small business of its own: salaries come in, rent or EMIs go out, bills fall due on different dates, insurance policies renew once a year and nobody remembers where the property papers are kept. Most families keep all of this in their heads, in a diary or in scattered phone notes.',
                'This toolkit gives every one of those jobs a ready Excel workbook that does the adding up for you: a monthly budget with a savings rate, a loan schedule that shows what a prepayment saves, a bills calendar, an index of family documents and policies, a register for the people who help at home, and an inventory for insurance claims. Everything stays on your own computer. It is a record-keeping kit, not financial advice.'],
         who='Families and couples, people living on their own, working parents, retired people managing a pension, and anyone who looks after the household paperwork.'),
]

ITEMS = []
def item(**k): ITEMS.append(dict(kit='home-money', **k))

item(id='hm-household-budget-excel', kind='excel', icon='🏠', name='Monthly Household Budget (Excel)',
     title='Monthly Household Budget in Excel: Family Budget Planner with Savings Rate and Chart',
     desc='Free family budget workbook: plan fixed, variable and savings categories, log income and daily spends, and see plan vs actual, savings rate and a 12-month view with a chart.',
     files=[('HM-Household-Budget.xlsx', 'Household budget workbook')],
     previews={'HM-Household-Budget.xlsx': ('Month', 'A3:F28')},
     lead='Decide once how much the household means to spend on each thing, then jot down money as it comes in and goes out. The Month sheet compares plan with actual for every category, adds up fixed bills, everyday spending and the money moved to savings, and shows the share of income you kept against your own savings target. The Year sheet lays twelve months side by side so you can see which months are heavy (school fees, festivals, insurance renewals).',
     inside=['Settings: household name, currency symbol, the month to view, the first month of your year and your savings target in percent',
             'Budget sheet: up to 50 categories, each marked Fixed, Variable or Savings, with a monthly planned amount (18 common household categories filled in to start)',
             'Income sheet: date, source, earner and amount for salaries, pension, rent received, interest and side income',
             'Spending sheet with 1,000 ready rows: date, category from a drop-down, description, amount and how it was paid (cash, UPI, card, bank, auto-debit)',
             'Month sheet: income, spending, savings, money left over and savings rate, plan vs actual per category with overspends in red, and a plan-vs-actual chart',
             'Year sheet: every category month by month for 12 months, with income, total spending and savings rate for each month'],
     steps=['Open the Settings sheet. Type your household name and currency symbol, pick the month you want to see and type your savings target (for example 20%).',
            'Open the Budget sheet. Rename, delete or add categories so they match your home, mark each one Fixed, Variable or Savings and type the amount you plan to spend each month.',
            'Each time money comes in, add a row on the Income sheet with the date, source and amount.',
            'Each time you spend, add a row on the Spending sheet: date, pick the category, a few words and the amount. Money you move into a recurring deposit, SIP or emergency fund goes in a Savings category.',
            'Open the Month sheet to see where the month stands. Red rows are categories where you have spent more than planned.',
            'At the end of the month, change the month on the Settings sheet to start looking at the next one. Use the Year sheet to compare months.'],
     tips=['Fixed means the amount is the same every month (rent, EMI, school fees); Variable changes (groceries, fuel, electricity). Seeing them apart shows how much of the budget you can actually control.',
           'Write spends the same day, even small ones. Many people find a five-minute evening routine easier than sorting a month of UPI messages later.',
           'If a spend does not fit any category it is counted as "Unplanned" on the Month sheet; add a category on the Budget sheet if it keeps happening.',
           'Yearly payments such as insurance premiums or property tax can be budgeted as one-twelfth every month in a Savings category, so the month they fall due does not hurt.',
           'Yellow cells are for typing; grey cells calculate themselves and are protected (Review › Unprotect Sheet if you really must change one). Dates show in your computer\'s own date format.'],
     faqs=[('How is the savings rate worked out?', 'Savings rate = (income − spending on Fixed and Variable categories) ÷ income for that month. Money you move into Savings categories counts as saved, and so does money simply left in the account. The sheet shows it next to your own target.'),
           ('Can two people in the family use it?', 'Yes. Put the file in a shared OneDrive or Google Drive folder, or keep one copy and note the earner on the Income sheet and the payment mode on the Spending sheet.'),
           ('Does it work in any currency?', 'Yes. Type your currency symbol on the Settings sheet; it appears in the headings. Amounts are plain numbers with thousand separators, so they work for rupees, dollars, dirhams or euros alike.'),
           ('Is this financial advice?', 'No. The workbook only adds up the figures you enter. How much to save or where to invest is a personal decision; speak to a qualified adviser if you need guidance.')])

item(id='hm-loan-emi-excel', kind='excel', icon='🏦', name='Loan & EMI Tracker with Prepayment (Excel)',
     title='Loan EMI Calculator and Amortisation Schedule in Excel with Prepayment Effect',
     desc='Free Excel loan tracker: EMI, month-by-month amortisation schedule, the effect of a one-time or monthly prepayment on interest and tenure, paid EMIs, and a list of all your loans.',
     files=[('HM-Loan-EMI-Tracker.xlsx', 'Loan and EMI tracker workbook')],
     previews={'HM-Loan-EMI-Tracker.xlsx': ('Loan', 'A3:C22')},
     lead='Type the loan amount, interest rate, tenure and first EMI date: the workbook works out the EMI and writes the full amortisation schedule, showing for every month how much of the EMI goes to interest and how much reduces the loan. Add a one-time prepayment or a small extra amount every month and see at once how many months it cuts and how much interest it saves. Mark each EMI as paid to always know the balance still owed.',
     inside=['Loan sheet: loan amount, yearly interest rate, tenure in months (up to 30 years), first EMI date, an extra monthly payment and a one-time prepayment in any EMI month',
             'Results: EMI, total interest without prepayment, total interest with prepayment, interest saved, months saved, the date the loan closes and the balance still owed today',
             'Schedule sheet with up to 360 rows: EMI date, opening balance, interest, principal, prepayment, closing balance and interest paid so far',
             '"Paid on" column to tick off EMIs as they are debited; paid rows turn green and prepayment months are highlighted',
             'All loans sheet: every loan in the family (home, vehicle, personal, education, gold, credit card) with outstanding amount, EMI, EMI date and end date, total EMIs and the share of monthly income they take'],
     steps=['On the Loan sheet type the loan name, currency symbol, loan amount, interest rate per year, tenure in months and the date of the first EMI.',
            'Check the EMI against your loan sanction letter. A small difference of a few units is normal: banks round in their own way.',
            'To see the effect of paying extra, type an amount in "Extra payment every month" or a one-time prepayment and the EMI number in which you will pay it.',
            'Compare the results: interest saved, months saved and the new closing date.',
            'Each month, type the date the EMI was paid in the "Paid on" column of the Schedule sheet. "Balance still owed" on the Loan sheet follows the last paid row.',
            'List all your loans on the All loans sheet to see the total EMI and how much of your monthly income it uses.'],
     tips=['The schedule assumes a fixed rate and that a prepayment shortens the tenure while the EMI stays the same, which is how most lenders apply part-payments by default. If your lender reduces the EMI instead, the interest saved will be lower.',
           'Floating-rate loans change rate over time. When your lender revises the rate, start a new copy from the current outstanding balance and remaining months.',
           'Ask your lender about prepayment charges and whether there is a minimum part-payment amount before you plan one.',
           'Make one copy of the file per loan (File › Save As) and keep the All loans sheet in the copy you open most.',
           'This is a calculator and record, not advice on whether to prepay or invest; consider your emergency fund and talk to a qualified adviser if unsure.'],
     faqs=[('How is the EMI calculated?', 'With the standard reducing-balance formula used by banks: EMI = P × r × (1 + r)^n ÷ ((1 + r)^n − 1), where P is the loan amount, r the monthly rate (yearly rate ÷ 12) and n the number of months. Excel\'s PMT function does exactly this.'),
           ('Why is most of my early EMI going to interest?', 'Interest is charged on the balance still owed. At the start the balance is highest, so interest takes the larger share; as the balance falls, more of each EMI goes to principal. The Schedule sheet shows this month by month.'),
           ('Can I enter more than one prepayment?', 'The Loan sheet takes one one-time prepayment plus a fixed extra amount every month. For several irregular prepayments, keep a copy of the file per scenario, or ask for a longer version through the contact page.'),
           ('Does it work for car, personal and education loans?', 'Yes, for any loan with equal monthly instalments and a fixed rate. It is not meant for credit card balances, which charge interest differently.')])

item(id='hm-bills-due-excel', kind='excel', icon='📅', name='Bills & Subscriptions Due-Date Tracker (Excel)',
     title='Bill Due Date Tracker in Excel: Bills, Subscriptions and Renewals Calendar',
     desc='Free Excel tracker for household bills and subscriptions: next due date for monthly, quarterly and yearly bills, overdue and due-soon alerts, upcoming list, 12-month cash calendar.',
     files=[('HM-Bills-and-Subscriptions.xlsx', 'Bills and subscriptions workbook')],
     previews={'HM-Bills-and-Subscriptions.xlsx': ('Upcoming', 'A3:F12')},
     lead='Electricity every month, broadband every quarter, the streaming app once a year, the car insurance on some date in March: list each bill once with how often it comes and when it was last paid, and the workbook works out the next due date, counts the days left and flags anything overdue or due soon. An Upcoming sheet lists the next bills in date order, and a 12-month calendar shows how much money each month needs. Mark subscriptions you want to review or cancel and see what they cost in a year.',
     inside=['Bills sheet: bill or subscription, category, provider, consumer or account number, amount, frequency (monthly, every 2 months, quarterly, every 4 months, half-yearly, yearly or one-time), first due date, last paid date, auto-pay and paid-from',
             'Next due date, days left and status (Overdue, Due today, Due soon, OK) calculated for every bill',
             'Yearly and monthly cost of every bill, and a Keep / Review / Cancel column for subscriptions',
             'Upcoming sheet: the next 30 bills in date order with amount, days left and auto-pay',
             'Calendar sheet: 12 months side by side showing which bills fall due in which month, with the total needed each month and a chart',
             'Summary: yearly cost of all bills, monthly average, overdue and due-soon counts, and the yearly cost of the subscriptions marked Review or Cancel'],
     steps=['On the Settings sheet type your currency symbol and how many days ahead you want a "Due soon" warning (7 to start with). "As on" is today unless you type another date.',
            'On the Bills sheet replace the sample bills with your own: name, category, amount, how often it comes and the first due date you know of (any past due date works).',
            'Each time you pay a bill, type the payment date in "Last paid on". The next due date moves forward by itself.',
            'Open the Upcoming sheet every week or so to see what is due next.',
            'Use the Calendar sheet to plan months with several big bills, and the Keep / Review / Cancel column to decide which subscriptions are worth keeping.'],
     tips=['For bills whose amount changes (electricity, water), type the usual amount; update it when the bill arrives if you want the calendar to be exact.',
           'Auto-pay bills still deserve a glance: a failed debit can mean a late fee. Keep the "Pay from" column so you know which account must have money.',
           'Only note the last 4 digits of account or card numbers. Never store passwords, PINs or OTPs in this file.',
           'Bills due on the 29th, 30th or 31st move to the last day of shorter months, as most billers do.'],
     faqs=[('How does it work out the next due date?', 'It starts from the first due date and steps forward by the bill\'s frequency until it passes the date you last paid. If you have not paid yet, the first due date itself is the next due date, so an unpaid old bill shows as Overdue.'),
           ('Does it send reminders?', 'No. Excel files cannot send messages. Open the Upcoming sheet regularly, or use the dates to set reminders in your phone calendar.'),
           ('What about weekly payments?', 'This tracker works in months. For a weekly payment, such as a newspaper or a milk bill paid weekly, enter the monthly total as a monthly bill.'),
           ('Can I use it for yearly renewals like insurance or the car service?', 'Yes. Choose Yearly as the frequency. For documents and insurance policies with more detail, the Family Documents & Policies Index in this toolkit is a better fit.')])

item(id='hm-family-documents-excel', kind='excel', icon='🗄️', name='Family Documents & Policies Index (Excel)',
     title='Family Document Organizer in Excel: Where Each Document Is Kept, Renewals and Policies',
     desc='Free Excel index of family documents and insurance policies: where each original and digital copy is kept, expiry and renewal dates, premiums, nominees and emergency contacts.',
     files=[('HM-Family-Documents-Index.xlsx', 'Family documents and policies workbook')],
     previews={'HM-Family-Documents-Index.xlsx': ('Documents', 'A3:L10')},
     lead='When a passport is about to expire, a claim must be filed or someone in the family falls ill, the question is always the same: where is the paper? This workbook is a map of the family\'s important documents and insurance policies. For each one it records who it belongs to, where the original is kept, where the scanned copy is, the nominee and the renewal date, and it warns you before anything expires. It deliberately has no place for passwords or PINs.',
     inside=['Members sheet: each family member with relation, date of birth, blood group and phone',
             'Documents sheet: member, document type from a list (identity cards, passport, driving licence, birth and marriage certificates, property papers, vehicle papers, bank and deposit records, will, medical and education records, tax papers, warranties), last 4 digits of the number, issued by, issue and expiry dates, where the original is kept, where the digital copy is, nominee and notes',
             'Status for every document: Valid, Renew soon (within your warning period), Expired or No expiry',
             'Policies sheet: health, life, vehicle, home, travel and accident policies with insurer, sum insured, premium and frequency, renewal date, nominee, agent or helpline and where the policy is kept; yearly premium and renewal status calculated',
             'Contacts sheet for the people to call in an emergency: family doctor, hospital, insurance helplines, bank, tax adviser, lawyer, society office',
             'Summary sheet: expired and due-soon counts, total yearly premium, sum insured by policy type, and the next 10 document expiries and policy renewals in date order'],
     steps=['On the Settings sheet set how many days before expiry you want a warning (60 to start with) and type your currency symbol.',
            'List the family on the Members sheet.',
            'Go through your files and add one row per document on the Documents sheet: whose it is, what it is, where the original is kept (for example "Steel almirah, top shelf, blue folder" or "Bank locker 214") and where the scanned copy is saved.',
            'Add every insurance policy on the Policies sheet with its renewal date and premium.',
            'Fill in the Contacts sheet with the numbers your family would need in a hurry.',
            'Look at the Summary sheet once a month and renew anything marked Renew soon or Expired. Tell at least one other family member where this file is.'],
     tips=['Never type passwords, PINs, CVV numbers, OTPs or full account and card numbers in this file. The last 4 digits are enough to recognise a document.',
           'If you store the file in the cloud, protect the account with two-step verification. You can also add a password to the file itself in Excel (File › Info › Protect Workbook › Encrypt with Password); keep that password somewhere safe outside the file.',
           'Print the Contacts sheet and keep it with the originals so the family can use it even without a computer.',
           'Review nominees whenever there is a marriage, birth or death in the family.'],
     faqs=[('Why does it only ask for the last 4 digits of numbers?', 'The file is meant to help you find documents, not to replace them. Keeping full identity, account or card numbers in a spreadsheet makes it valuable to anyone who gets hold of it.'),
           ('Can I add more document types?', 'Yes. The types are in a list on the hidden Lists sheet (right-click any sheet tab › Unhide › Lists). Add yours at the end of the list and they appear in the drop-down.'),
           ('How is the yearly premium calculated?', 'Premium × payments per year: 1 for yearly, 2 for half-yearly, 4 for quarterly and 12 for monthly. Single-premium policies count as 0 per year.'),
           ('Is this a legal record?', 'No. It is an index that tells you where the real documents are. The original documents and the insurer\'s records are what count.')])

item(id='hm-house-help-excel', kind='excel', icon='🧹', name='House Help Salary & Advance Register (Excel)',
     title='Maid, Cook and Driver Salary Register in Excel: Leaves, Advances and Payment Receipt',
     desc='Free Excel register for domestic workers: monthly salary with paid leaves and leave deduction, festival bonus, advances given and recovered, yearly summary and a printable receipt.',
     files=[('HM-House-Help-Register.xlsx', 'House help salary register workbook')],
     previews={'HM-House-Help-Register.xlsx': ('Pay register', 'A3:M9')},
     lead='The cook, the cleaner, the driver, the nanny: paying them fairly and on time is easier with a written record that both sides can trust. List each person once with their monthly salary and paid leaves; each month enter the leaves taken, any bonus and any advance being recovered, and the register works out what to pay. Advances given and recovered are tracked person by person, and a receipt sheet prints a payment slip to be signed.',
     inside=['Staff sheet: name, work (cook, cleaning, driver, nanny, gardener, guard, elder care), phone, address, start date, monthly salary, paid leaves per month, ID proof and police verification noted, emergency contact',
             'Pay register: one row per person per month with leaves taken, extra leaves beyond the allowance, leave deduction worked out on the days of that month, bonus or overtime, advance recovered and net amount paid, with date and mode of payment',
             'Advances sheet: date, person, amount given, reason and the planned monthly recovery',
             'Summary sheet: for each person, salary, months paid and amount paid this year, advances given, recovered and still outstanding',
             'Receipt sheet: pick a name and month to print a payment receipt with a signature line'],
     steps=['On the Settings sheet type your household name, currency symbol and the first month of your year (for the yearly totals).',
            'On the Staff sheet list each person with their monthly salary and how many paid leaves a month you have agreed.',
            'When you give an advance, add a row on the Advances sheet.',
            'At salary time, add one row per person on the Pay register: the month, the name, leaves taken, any bonus and any advance you are recovering this month. Check the net amount, pay it and type the date and mode.',
            'Open the Receipt sheet, pick the name and month, print it and ask the person to sign. Keep the signed copy.',
            'Look at the Summary sheet to see how much advance is still to be recovered from each person.'],
     tips=['Agree on salary, paid leaves, weekly off and notice period at the start and write them in the Notes column; most disputes come from things never said.',
           'The leave deduction is worked out as monthly salary ÷ days in that month × extra leaves. If you have agreed a different rule (for example no deduction at all), type 0 leaves or adjust with the Bonus column and add a note.',
           'Recover advances in small, agreed amounts rather than all at once.',
           'Rules on minimum wages, registration and social security for domestic workers differ by country and state; check what applies where you live.',
           'Paying by UPI or bank transfer leaves a record for both of you; note the mode in the register either way.'],
     faqs=[('How is the leave deduction calculated?', 'Extra leaves = leaves taken − paid leaves allowed (never below zero). Deduction = monthly salary ÷ number of days in that month × extra leaves, rounded to a whole amount. A 30-day month and a 31-day month therefore give slightly different daily rates.'),
           ('How do I record an advance?', 'Add the amount you gave on the Advances sheet. In each later month type the part you are recovering in the "Advance recovered" column of the Pay register; the Summary sheet shows what is still outstanding.'),
           ('Can I pay half a month to someone who joined mid-month?', 'Yes. In that month\'s row type a negative amount in the Bonus column for the days not worked (for example −3,500 for half of a 7,000 salary) and write the reason in the Note column.'),
           ('Is this a legal employment contract?', 'No. It is a payment record. If you want a written agreement, keep it as a separate letter signed by both of you; this register helps show that payments were made as agreed.')])

item(id='hm-home-inventory-excel', kind='excel', icon='🛋️', name='Home Inventory for Insurance (Excel)',
     title='Home Inventory Spreadsheet in Excel: Household Contents List for Insurance Claims',
     desc='Free Excel home contents inventory: items room by room with brand, serial number, purchase date and price, warranty, receipt and photo checks, and totals by room and category.',
     files=[('HM-Home-Inventory.xlsx', 'Home inventory workbook')],
     previews={'HM-Home-Inventory.xlsx': ('Summary', 'A3:D20')},
     lead='After a fire, flood or theft, an insurer asks for a list of what was lost, what it cost and proof that you owned it. Few people can write that list from memory on the worst day of the year. This workbook lets you walk through the home room by room once, noting each valuable item with its brand, serial number, purchase date, price and where the bill is kept, and checks whether you have a photo and a receipt for each. Totals by room and category tell you whether your contents cover is in the right range.',
     inside=['Settings: household and address, currency symbol, insurer and contents policy number, and your list of rooms',
             'Items sheet with 500 rows: room, item, category, brand and model, serial number, purchase date, quantity, price paid, estimated value now, warranty end date, where the receipt is kept, photo taken and receipt kept',
             'Calculated for every item: total paid, value for a claim, warranty status and age in years, and whether proof is complete',
             'Summary sheet: items, amount paid and value by room and by category, total value, items under warranty, share of items with full proof, and a chart by room'],
     steps=['On the Settings sheet type your address, currency symbol, insurer and policy number, and change the list of rooms to match your home.',
            'Walk through one room at a time. For each item worth listing, add a row: room, item, category, brand and model, serial number (usually on a sticker at the back or bottom), purchase date and price.',
            'Take a photo of each item and its serial number sticker, and mark "Photo taken" Yes. Mark "Receipt kept" Yes when you have the bill and note where it is.',
            'If an item is old, type an estimated value now; otherwise the price paid is used.',
            'Check the Summary sheet: the total value is a rough guide to the contents sum insured you may want to discuss with your insurer.',
            'Add new purchases the day you buy them, and save a copy of the file outside the home (cloud storage or with a relative).'],
     tips=['Jewellery, watches, art and collectibles often have limits in a standard contents policy; list them separately and ask your insurer whether they need to be declared.',
           'A short video walking through each room, with cupboards opened, is a quick extra record alongside this list.',
           'Keep the photos and scanned bills in one folder named after the room, and write that folder in "Receipt kept at".',
           'Warranty dates help even without a claim: the Summary shows how many items are still under warranty before you pay for a repair.'],
     faqs=[('Which items should I list?', 'Anything that would be costly or hard to replace: electronics, appliances, furniture, jewellery, watches, bicycles, musical instruments, good clothes and kitchenware sets. Small everyday items can go as one row per group, for example "Books, about 150".'),
           ('What value should I use?', 'The price paid is always recorded. Insurers pay either the replacement cost or the depreciated value, depending on the policy, so the "Estimated value now" column is optional; your policy wording decides what a claim pays.'),
           ('Is my data uploaded anywhere?', 'No. The file stays on your computer or wherever you save it. Keep a copy outside the home so it survives the event you are insuring against.'),
           ('Does this replace my insurance documents?', 'No. It supports a claim; the insurer\'s policy, the bills and the photos are the evidence.')])

item(id='hm-home-budget-planner', kind='online', tool='home-budget', icon='⚡', name='Online Monthly Budget Planner',
     title='Online Monthly Budget Planner: Household Budget in Any Currency, Print or Download',
     desc='Plan a monthly household budget online in any currency: income, fixed, variable and savings spends, plan vs actual, savings rate against your target. Print, Excel or Word. Private.',
     lead='A quick monthly budget without opening Excel. Pick your currency, list the month\'s income and your spending categories with what you plan to spend and what you actually spent. The planner adds up fixed bills, everyday spending and money moved to savings, shows what is left over and your savings rate against your target, and gives you a tidy page to print or download as Excel or Word. Your household name, currency and target are remembered on this device; nothing is uploaded.',
     inside=['Currency choice (rupee, dollar, euro, pound, dirham, riyal and more) with the right number style for it',
             'Date style choice: DD-MM-YYYY, MM-DD-YYYY or YYYY-MM-DD',
             'Income lines for every earner and source',
             'Spending categories marked Fixed, Variable or Savings, each with a planned and an actual amount, and the difference',
             'Totals: income, fixed, variable, savings, left over and savings rate compared with your target',
             'Print / Save as PDF, Excel and Word downloads; works offline once the site is installed as an app'],
     steps=['Type your household name, choose your currency and date style and set a savings target in percent. These stay saved on this device.',
            'Pick the month.',
            'Add each income: salary, pension, rent received, side income.',
            'Add your spending categories with type, planned amount and, as the month goes on, the actual amount.',
            'Read the totals and the savings rate in the preview, then print it or download it as Excel or Word.'],
     tips=['Start with last month: fill in actual amounts from your bank and UPI history and use them to set realistic plans for this month.',
           'Press "New budget (keep my details)" at the start of each month; your categories need to be typed again, so download last month\'s Excel copy to keep a record.',
           'For daily spend logging and a 12-month view, use the Monthly Household Budget workbook in this toolkit.'],
     faqs=[('Is my budget sent anywhere?', 'No. It is worked out in your browser. Your details and the current draft are saved only in this browser on this device; clearing site data removes them.'),
           ('How is the savings rate calculated?', '(Total income − Fixed − Variable spending) ÷ total income. Money moved to Savings categories and money left over both count as saved.'),
           ('Can I use it outside India?', 'Yes. Choose your currency and date style; amounts are shown in that currency\'s usual format.'),
           ('Does it give advice on how to save or invest?', 'No. It is a planning and record-keeping aid that adds up your own figures.')])
